---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Use cases"
  description="Where DingoDB’s document-native model, deterministic mathematical kernel, indexed speed, and explicit damage semantics create unusual leverage."
  path="/use-cases/"
  eyebrow="Fit"
  lead="Choose DingoDB when flexible documents, formal truth, direct query mechanics, speed, and survival belong in one system."
>
  <h2>Good fit to evaluate now</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Document-native Rust applications</strong> that want an ordinary
      collection API over deterministic query and examination semantics
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>High-ingest local systems</strong> that need live indexes,
      explicit durability modes, history, and bounded resource behavior
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Systems that cannot lie about partial data</strong>—coverage,
      holes, conflicts, and recovery evidence must remain visible
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Teams exploring mathematical document integrity</strong> through
      DRE, Atomics, direct rank access, and counted ordering as those profiles
      move from specification to implementation
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Callout title="Experimental / early access" variant="warning">
    <p>
      Even good-fit workloads should treat embedded single-node as early access.
      Interfaces, formats, and guarantees may change. See
      <a href="/status/">status</a>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Evaluate carefully</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Applications that may need a network path soon but can start embedded</li>
    <li>Teams comparing with SQLite for local documents, richer query semantics, and salvage</li>
    <li>Teams comparing with MongoDB that can adopt DingoDB incrementally as the rule and direct-access profiles land</li>
    <li>Operators exploring single-node TCP for development only</li>
    <li>Workloads that need long-term retention design before native archive tiers exist</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Not yet</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A drop-in mature SQL database</li>
    <li>A production drop-in MongoDB replacement</li>
    <li>A public multi-tenant service requiring mature network operations</li>
    <li>A production multi-node cluster</li>
    <li>A native object-store archive platform</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Decision shortcuts</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Decision shortcuts" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">If you need…</th>
          <th scope="col">Today</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Flexible documents + deterministic kernel + damage honesty</td>
          <td>Evaluate DingoDB embedded (experimental)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Database-enforced document rules, exact from/to rank, or wavelet ordering</td>
          <td>Follow DingoDB’s normative designs; implementation is not complete</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mature SQL + ecosystem</td>
          <td>PostgreSQL or SQLite</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mature document DB operations</td>
          <td>MongoDB or similar</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Production network cluster</td>
          <td>Do not choose DingoDB yet</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="btn-row">
    <a href="/status/" class="btn btn-primary">Evaluate against status</a>
    <a href="/" class="btn btn-secondary">Back to home</a>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
